{"kind":"report","text":"Implementation review of item 1 (host-level binding contract and vocabulary) at exact head 8d5f9ee6391985a23988dd1239ff3ac50cc1f867 on request/host-binding-vocabulary, against docs candidate cf44407644a2195af05c67f7bb82bf42381438ca. Local and origin tips both at that commit; worktree clean. I reviewed the design this implements across four rounds and approved it at ad3dfd7a (report bac34200), so I am holding this head to that design's own words.\n\nVERDICT: CHANGES-REQUESTED. The scope discipline is exemplary and the substance is a faithful implementation of the item-1 contract I approved. Three defects stop it, all in the contract text itself, all small: the page now contradicts itself on a count, the new vocabulary has no identifier, and the security property that makes an untrusted URL safe to record is missing. None requires rework of the approach.\n\n=== A. STRICT SCOPE: CONFIRMED ===\n\nOnly docs/reference/architecture.md changed. `git diff --name-only main...8d5f9ee6` returns that one path, 61 insertions and 12 deletions. No code, no tests, no other documents.\n\nItem-1 only, with no item-2 leak. The head is contract text and nothing else, and it says so twice in the right places: the new section closes with \"This section defines the contract only. The following implementation item adds binding and interpreter selection behavior in `internal/app`\", and the `internal/app` package row is amended to \"It will consume the host binding and select one interpreter; that behavior is outside this contract-only head.\" That is the correct way to hold a seam open without acting on it. No initialization behavior, no selection code, no registry, no plugin loader, no per-event routing, no backfill.\n\nTrailers verified by reading the commit, not the summary. The single commit 8d5f9ee6 carries exactly two Rests-On lines: the fresh request 3b7ad7d682fb6654035d3a0bf1806313d655a54d and the ratified basis b15de2f8788788a1afe970d6d077f7843862ebf2. It does not carry the historical d5d30c17 and does not rest on the stale request d3943090. Condition (3) of the implementation request is met exactly. The historical filing d5d30c17 appears once, in prose, explicitly labelled \"cited here as design history, not as this page's causal basis\" — which is the distinction the request asked for, stated on the page rather than only in the commit.\n\nFaithfulness to the design's item 1, checked clause by clause against the note I approved. The four binding fields are all present and correctly characterised: application name; source commit \"as a format-qualified object ID\"; the URL \"as provenance, never as authority\"; and the fold-profile version or hash. The positional rule is there: \"effective only in the repository's bootstrap position, or as a later replacement signed by the key that initialized the repository. A record that merely resembles a binding anywhere else has no force.\" The order is there and is stated as fixed: \"read the binding, select the named interpreter, then fold\", with the explicit prohibition on folding with a guess and repairing afterwards. The legacy rule is there: no binding means Workroom at the shipped version, with bootstrap-operator authority. The honest failure is there: \"verifiable but uninterpretable\". And the authority asymmetry I specifically asked the design to write down rather than leave to discovery is here in full, including its reason: \"retiring an operator inside Workroom does not revoke the initializing key's binding authority, because another application has no Workroom roster to consult.\" That is Q1 and Q2 of my design review landed correctly.\n\nArtifact path. The candidate cf444076 is recorded at path `docs`, not at `docs/reference/architecture.md`. That is correct rather than a defect: every artifact ever recorded at `docs/reference/architecture.md` is retired, and `docs` is the granularity this area has settled on, so the candidate matches the string the area keeps using. I checked the projection rather than assuming.\n\n=== B. WHAT BLOCKS ===\n\nF1 (blocking, one word) — the page now contradicts itself on the number of compatibility axes. The heading still reads \"## Compatibility has five axes\" while the table below it now lists six: Kernel protocol, Host binding, Application family, Interpreter or fold, Projection contract, Surface or UI. I verified this is introduced here rather than pre-existing: on main the heading says five and the table has exactly five data rows; at this head the table has six and the heading is untouched. This is the one section the review conditions asked me to judge for coherence, and it is currently wrong on its face to any reader who counts. Fix: \"six axes\".\n\nF2 (blocking) — the new vocabulary has no identifier, so the layer cannot do the job the section says it exists to do. Section 4 justifies the layer precisely on cross-application readability: the vocabulary sits above the kernel and below every profile \"because a host must read it without already knowing whether the repository contains Workroom, chess, or another application.\" A host can only do that if the binding is identifiable by a fixed schema string that every application agrees on, and the page never says what that string is. Every other layer on this page names its family: section 5 names the `workroom/*` schemas, and the compatibility table's Application-family axis names `workroom/*` as its marker. The new Host-binding axis names no marker at all, describing behaviour instead (\"Legacy absence selects shipped Workroom; explicit bindings are host-level records\"), and the new package-table row is titled \"Host binding vocabulary\" with no package path, uniquely among rows that otherwise all name a real home. A schema family is contract, not behaviour, so this belongs in item 1 rather than item 2 — and without it, item 2 may choose any string and no reviewer will have a contract to check it against. Two applications that pick differently cannot read each other's bindings, which is the exact failure the layer was created to prevent. Fix: name the host-binding schema family on the page, and give the axis row that marker.\n\nF3 (blocking, security) — the contract records an untrusted URL and never says that nothing may act on it. The design I approved carries this as its own core decision: \"Recording a binding runs no code, and no gitseq command fetches, builds, or runs application code as a side effect\", and that property is what makes it safe to write an attacker-suppliable URL into a permanent record. I grepped the whole page at this head: there is no statement about executing, fetching, or building application code anywhere. \"Provenance, never as authority\" establishes that the URL confers no trust, which is necessary but not sufficient — it does not forbid a host from fetching it for convenience, and a supply-chain fetch triggered by opening a repository is precisely the risk the design closed. This is contract, not behaviour, and it is the security half of the binding contract. Fix: state on the page that reading or recording a binding never fetches, builds, or runs application code.\n\n=== C. NON-BLOCKING NOTES ===\n\n1. Tie-break unspecified. \"Effective only in the repository's bootstrap position\" does not say what happens if two binding-shaped records occupy that position — first wins, last wins, or both are void. A hostile or buggy bootstrap can produce that, and item 2 will have to decide it silently if the contract does not. Worth one clause.\n2. The kernel's \"does not understand\" list in section 2 could name the binding for symmetry. It is coherent without it, since the kernel binds \"an opaque schema name and opaque payload tree\" and the new section says the vocabulary sits above the kernel, so a binding is opaque to it by construction. Optional.\n\n=== D. THE THREE CONCLUSIONS ===\n\nARCHITECTURE. Layers affected: the flow diagram (Host inserted between the verified stream and the application subgraph, with Stream to Host and Host to each interpreter, correctly rewired); a new layer 4, Application host binding; the former layers 4, 5 and 6 renumbered to 5, 6 and 7; the compatibility table, which gains a Host binding axis and narrows Application family to \"interpreted after host selection\"; and the package table, which gains a Host binding vocabulary row and amends the `internal/app` row. I checked for stale cross-references to the old numbering across docs/ and the repository root and found none, so the renumbering is self-consistent.\n\nJudged as a complete contract, which is what the conditions asked: it is coherent in structure and nearly complete in substance, but not complete. The layer is placed correctly, the dependency-direction paragraph is updated to say the binding \"belongs at that seam, not in either lower package or inside a particular application\", and the ordering rule is unambiguous. What a second application actually needs in order to implement against this page is the identifier (F2) and the no-execution guarantee (F3); with those two sentences the contract stands on its own, and without them a chess implementer must guess at the one thing the layer exists to standardise. The same-head requirement is otherwise satisfied: the page is updated in the head that changes the contract, and the candidate artifact is published at that head.\n\nSECURITY. Boundaries examined: binding authority, the cross-application trust boundary, pinned source identity, the untrusted URL, the unavailable-interpreter failure, legacy authority, secrets, and bounds. Correct and explicit: authority is bound to the initializing key and stated as a host fact below application roles, with the reason given, so no application-level role change can revoke it and no application can claim it; the source is a format-qualified object ID rather than a bare hash, which is the identity discipline I asked for in design review and which forecloses ambiguity across object formats; the unavailable interpreter degrades to verifiable-but-uninterpretable rather than to a guess. No secrets are introduced and no bounds are changed, correctly for a contract-only head. The one unresolved security item is F3. I found no privilege the binding grants that the initializing key did not already hold, which is what the design claimed and this page preserves.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Nothing to cut. One layer, four fields, one ordering rule, one legacy rule — this is the minimum that expresses item 1, and the head resisted every available temptation: no registry, no plugin loader, no per-event routing, no backfill, no runtime code, and no speculative interface for item 2. The `internal/app` row is amended rather than rewritten, and the deferral is stated instead of implemented. The two additions I am requesting in F2 and F3 are each a sentence and neither adds a mechanism.\n\n=== E. GATES, RUN INDEPENDENTLY AT THE EXACT HEAD ===\n\nAll five green, run in the request worktree at 8d5f9ee6 with a clean tree, serialized against other work after confirming no other go test was running:\n\n- git diff --check main...HEAD: clean\n- go vet ./...: exit 0\n- go build ./...: exit 0\n- go test ./... -count=1: exit 0 (all packages ok; internal/kernel 137.5s, internal/service 25.9s)\n- go test ./internal/docset/... -count=1: ok, 83.345s, exit 0\n\nThe docset caveat I have reported in all four design reviews still applies: internal/docset walks docs/ only. Unlike this head's predecessors that caveat cuts in its favour here, because this head changes a docs page and the gate therefore does exercise it. It passes, including the citation-anchor checks that were failing on the stale second-application branch.\n\n=== F. WHAT WOULD MAKE THIS AN APPROVAL ===\nChange \"five axes\" to six; name the host-binding schema family on the page and use it as the axis marker; and state that reading or recording a binding never fetches, builds, or runs application code. Nothing else. The scope discipline, the trailers, the fidelity to the approved design, and all five gates are already right, and I would approve the next head on those three edits alone.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:cf44407644a2195af05c67f7bb82bf42381438ca","head":"8d5f9ee6391985a23988dd1239ff3ac50cc1f867","verdict":"changes-requested"}}